In 1992, Dynamix and Sierra On-Line released *Johnny Castaway*. Whenever an office PC went idle, a tiny castaway fished, slept, built rafts, and got into trouble on Windows 3.1. And for decades, that is pretty much where he stayed. Enjoying that casual rhythm today usually means dragging a beige tower out of a closet or coaxing an old Windows install inside a virtual machine. Because the friction is so high, almost nobody bothers.

I wanted Johnny where I actually spend my time, so [he now runs in a browser tab](https://khawkins98.github.io/johnny_web/). Rebuilding that environment was only possible with AI assistance. Using LLMs to reverse-engineer old binaries has become a popular weekend hack lately, but when I started this work, the path was much less charted—and it was rarely easy. It meant decoding custom bytecode, reconstructing a lost scene director, and building an automated oracle to hold the browser port to the behavior of the original 16-bit executable.

> **tl;dr**
>
> - Johnny's island combines scripted gags and weighted random choices with an eleven-day story that advances at most one step per calendar day.
> - The browser port builds on earlier reverse-engineering work and interprets the original game resources in JavaScript.
> - Running the original Windows program under instrumentation provides reference traces; separate rendering “goldens” catch changes in the port.

## Inside a gag, and between gags

The original files contain artwork, sound, and small programs describing the animations. They use the [Dynamix Game Development System (DGDS)](https://github.com/scummvm/scummvm/tree/master/engines/dgds), Sierra's proprietary adventure engine. My browser implementation, [Bottle DGDS](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/docs/architecture.md#system-overview), parses those resources and runs their bytecode instructions in JavaScript.

The scripts operate on two levels:

- **[ADS (Animation Director Script)](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/docs/architecture.md#resource-and-script-model) files coordinate a gag.** They act as the high-level sequencer: starting and stopping scenes, evaluating random branches, and waiting for other actions to finish.
- **[TTM (Tiny Templated Movie)](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/docs/architecture.md#resource-and-script-model) files perform the animation.** They handle per-frame work: loading bitmap sprites, drawing sprite frames, waiting on timer ticks, playing sound samples, and looping.

The [“Eight Arm Menace” scene flow](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/docs/scene-flows/FISHING.ADS.md#eight-arm-menace-fishingads-tag-3) shows how they fit together: Johnny gets his pole, casts, reels in a heavy catch, and reaches the octopus gag. Several animation threads run concurrently. Their relative order and timing determine whether the result lands as a coherent joke.

Deciding which gag comes next is a separate problem. In 1992, the Windows executable wrapped a scene director around these scripts. It picked an eligible story scene, queued up everyday filler scenes before it, and scheduled Johnny's walks between their starting and ending positions. The browser port rebuilds that [scene-selection behaviour](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/docs/johnny-host-behavior.md) outside the script interpreter.

That separation explained why earlier web ports felt incomplete. They could parse and play individual animations, but without the director, ordinary playback never selected them. Decoding a file tells you how to render an animation, but not when Johnny should decide to walk across the beach.

## Asking the original what it actually does

I didn't start from scratch. [Alexandre Fontoura's Castaway](https://github.com/xesf/castaway) provided a working JavaScript foundation for decoding resources. Jérémie Guillaume's [Johnny Reborn](https://github.com/jno6809/jc_reborn), Hans Milling's [C# remake](https://github.com/nivs1978/Johnny-Castaway-Open-Source), and ScummVM's [DGDS engine](https://github.com/scummvm/scummvm/tree/master/engines/dgds) offered crucial reference points.

Still, reverse engineering has an easy trap: an interpretation can look convincing on screen while being completely wrong under the hood. The only reliable benchmark is the original 1992 program running in real time.

To get that, I built [capture tooling](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/tools/faithfulness-oracle/METHODOLOGY.md) around Windows 3.1 running in a patched DOSBox-X emulator. Instead of dumping raw memory or monitoring operating system CPU threads, the harness hooks directly into the 16-bit executable's main script tick function. On each 50 Hz logical tick, it samples the engine's internal tables to log active script threads (the "actors"), their runstates, delay counters, and random-number generator choices into structured JSON Lines (`timeline.jsonl`) files.

