Checking the port against the original comes down to four steps:

1. Run a gag in the instrumented emulator multiple times to capture every random branch.
2. Distill those execution logs into reference fingerprints: structured JSON readouts capturing which script threads run concurrently, their peak count, and their lifespans.
3. Run the browser engine and compare its tick-by-tick JavaScript timeline against those reference fingerprints.
4. Investigate discrepancies, using visual checks whenever the traces diverge.

The traces immediately caught subtle bugs. For example, treating random branches equally made Johnny exit a fishing sequence about three times too often. The original data assigned different weights to each outcome. Both versions reached a plausible ending on screen, but the remake was choosing the wrong path far too frequently.

Animation timing had similar traps. How long an animation is allowed to run is distinct from how long each individual frame should pause. Mixing up those clocks altered how actions overlapped, even when the drawings themselves were correct.

A second safety net catches regressions: [rendering goldens](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/test/render-goldens.mjs). These replay fragile sequences with deterministic inputs and save compact fingerprints of drawing operations and pixels. A later code change that alters output makes the check fail, flagging the difference for review. The [coverage report](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/docs/oracle-coverage.md) records what has been verified and where gaps remain.

This is where AI proved invaluable. LLMs are remarkably good at suggesting what an obscure assembly routine or opcode table might be doing, but they are equally prone to plausible hallucinations. Running the original executable gave those suggestions an unyielding ground truth: an AI hypothesis either matched the emulator trace, or it was wrong.

## The machinery behind a living screensaver

Once the runtime faithfully executes the scripts, the elegance of Dynamix's original design becomes obvious. Jeff Tunnell, who co-founded Dynamix and created Johnny Castaway, [summarized the core idea](https://jefftunnell.com/johnny-castaway-common-questions/):

> “So it generally runs a little loop that is interrupted by random events.”

The variety operates across multiple interlocking clocks. Within a day, the director chooses among eligible everyday scenes and gags using weighted probabilities. Across days, it follows an [eleven-day story](https://github.com/khawkins98/johnny_web/blob/main/docs/story-over-time.md), with particular milestones tied to calendar dates.

